@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for Linux (64-bit)
========================================

Raspberry Pi: this needs the 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS. On the 32-bit one,
"Play @TITLE@" can't start (the system says it can't execute it, or that
the file isn't found). In a terminal, "getconf LONG_BIT" says 64 on a
64-bit system.

@TITLE@ (@STUDIO@), recompiled to run natively: the game's own program
translated into C++ and compiled for your computer, drawn with Vulkan.

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the game: @RELEASE@,
dumped from the GD-ROM as a .cue file with its .bin tracks (a Redump-style
dump, music tracks included), a .gdi with its tracks, or a .chd.


Playing
-------
1. Unpack the download anywhere you can write to (your home folder is
   fine): double-click it in your file manager, or in a terminal
   "tar xzf @PACKAGE@-*.tar.gz". Building the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open the @PACKAGE@ folder and double-click "Play @TITLE@" (if your file
   manager asks, choose Execute or Run). From a terminal: ./"Play @TITLE@"
3. Click "Choose your disc" and pick your disc's .cue, .gdi or .chd file,
   or drag the file onto the window. (Where the desktop has no file
   chooser, type or paste the file's path.) It tells you straight away if
   the disc is the wrong game or another version.
4. Wait for the first build: a few minutes on a desktop PC. Your game
   files never leave your computer.
5. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs. "Add to the
   applications menu" puts @TITLE@ in your desktop's menu.

After that, Play @TITLE@ remembers your disc (even in a new download
folder), and Play starts the game straight away. After an update it builds
the game again first, recompiling only what changed.

The game's output is in work/run.log and work/run-errors.log, and the
build's in work/build.log. Send them when reporting a problem.

Needs: a 64-bit Linux on x86-64 (the linux-x86_64 download) or ARM64 (the
linux-arm64 download) with glibc 2.35 or later (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12,
Fedora 36, Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm or later), and a graphics driver with
Vulkan 1.3: Mesa's Vulkan drivers (on Debian and Ubuntu, the
mesa-vulkan-drivers package) or NVIDIA's. Before building, Play @TITLE@
checks that there is one and says what to install if not. Updates use
curl (or wget) and tar, which nearly every Linux has.


Raspberry Pi
------------
- The 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS (see the top of this file), on a Raspberry Pi
  4 or 5 with 2 GB of memory or more. Use the linux-arm64 download.
- Vulkan 1.3 for the Pi's graphics arrived with Mesa 24.3: Raspberry Pi OS
  Trixie (October 2025) or later has it. On an older Raspberry Pi OS, Play
  @TITLE@ says so before building: update Raspberry Pi OS.
- The first build compiles the whole game on the Pi itself, so it takes a
  while: much longer than on a desktop PC, and longer on a Pi 4 than a Pi
  5, and on a 2 GB Pi, which compiles one file at a time. Leave it
  running. Later builds take compiled files from the cache.
- "Add to the applications menu" (once the game is built) puts @TITLE@ in
  the Raspberry Pi menu, under Games.


Controls
--------
A Dreamcast controller. Up to four players: the first controller (or the
keyboard) is port A, the others ports B to D.

  Controller           Keyboard        Dreamcast
  left stick           arrows          the analogue stick
  D-pad                I J K L         the D-pad
  A, B, X, Y           Z, X, A, S      A, B, X, Y
  LB, RB, triggers     Q, W            L, R
  Start                Enter           Start

Back + Start together, or F1 or Esc: the settings menu (the game pauses):
controls (set by pressing them), the window, the picture, the refresh
rate, enhancements and the Dreamcast's own settings (language, sound).
Alt+Enter or F11: full screen. A controller that rumbles plays the Jump Pack.

Settings and memory-card saves are kept in ~/.local/share/qilin/@GAME@ (or
$XDG_DATA_HOME/qilin/@GAME@), with the disc you chose and the build's cache
of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in here
--------------
Play @TITLE@        chooses your disc, builds the game (once) and starts it
kit/qilin           the recompiler: turns the disc's @BOOT@ into C++
kit/lib, include    the prebuilt rest of the game: its runtime, the renderer,
                    sound, the window and controls (SDL3), the menu (Dear ImGui)
kit/toolchain/zig   zig, a C++ compiler (Clang, with its own libc++)
kit/icon.png        the menu entry's icon
kit/files.txt       every file this release holds (see below)
work/               made when building: the game (@EXE_LINUX@), built from your
                    disc, the recompiled code, the build's files and the logs
LICENSES/           the licences of everything included

zig keeps its own cache of compiled libraries in ~/.cache/zig.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/Qilin1066/ToyCommander--QilinRecomp

Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in Play @TITLE@ asks GitHub for a newer release of
@TITLE@ (it sends nothing about you or your computer). If there is one, it
downloads it, unpacks it beside this folder and swaps in the new kit and
launcher, then starts again and builds the game for the new version. If
anything fails on the way, the old version is left as it was. You can also
unpack a new download anywhere, even over this folder: it finds your disc
and the cache by itself, and at its first start it removes files the new
kit no longer has (anything in kit/ or LICENSES/ that kit/files.txt doesn't
name). Your saves and settings are never in this folder, so nothing of
yours is touched.

The game is compiled for this computer's processor; moved to another
computer, it is built again. Compiled files come from the cache when
nothing they're made from has changed. A failed build leaves the working
game as it was; the one before a new build is kept as
work/@EXE_LINUX@.previous until the new one has run and closed normally.
"Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.

Before a save burst, the memory card is copied into its five-entry history;
flash updates keep their own history too. Use the settings menu's Game page
to restore a copy and restart. VMU packets are checked small writes; the
history supports recovery after an interrupted save, rather than making a
whole game save transactional. Existing unreadable or malformed card/flash
files stop the game and are left unchanged. Keep a separate copy before
manual recovery.

Sound and controller recovery
-----------------------------
If the sound output is unavailable, the game keeps running and retries it
once a second. Playback resumes with the selected volume and mute setting;
sound from the outage is discarded rather than played late. Recovery and
errors are recorded in run-errors.log.

A controller that pressed while all four ports were full can take a port
when another controller disconnects. Existing players keep their ports.
Vibration expires quickly if the game stops updating it.
